[test-suite name=http
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/http (spec/02-inprogress/stdlib_http.md §10).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/http']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$http:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result.

   LAYERING: cx-stdlib/http is an L7 HTTP/1.1 client+server built ON TOP of
   the L4 cx-stdlib/net transport. It is a Tier-B, necessarily-impure module
   for its network verbs and introduces NO new capability — every effect
   point is gated on `net` (§5). It owns CXER4525-4543 (E_HTTP_*), the next
   free block above net's CXER4500-4524 (governance §9.6).

   DETERMINISM / TWO LANES (mirrors net.cxd's authoring model):

   (1) GATED network verbs (get/post/put/del/patch/head/options/request/
   send; serve/listen/accept-iter/exchange-request/respond/stop) reach the
   network through net and are guarded fail-closed on the `net` capability.
   The stdlib runner is the host: a case whose expected output carries
   CXER0271 runs under the EMPTY capability set (deny-by-default per
   security.md §2), so the effect point denies and the verb short-circuits
   to the E_CAP_DENIED = cx-err:CXER0271 err VALUE (§4.1/§5). Real socket I/O
   (connect/bind/accept) is neither reproducible nor safe inside the harness,
   so AUTHORING.md ("Capability lanes") directs the capability-denied
   assertion for the happy-path call shape of every gated verb.

   (2) The capability-GRANTED behavior runs under a full grant (the runner
   grants all caps for any non-CXER0271 case). Behind the grant the verbs
   return the deterministic synthetic shapes the impl emits in the
   conformance/granted harness ([response status=200], an [http-server]
   handle, the parsed [request], null). These exercise the GRANTED call
   shape + arity without real sockets; live round-trips (real status/headers/
   body, TLS, redirects, decompression) belong in a network-granted harness.

   PURE introspection (status/ok/header/headers/headers-named/has-body/
   body-bytes/body-bytes-wire/body-text) and pool bookkeeping (client/close)
   are capability-free (§3.4/§3.1): they operate on a materialized,
   homoiconic [request]/[response] literal and produce real output under the
   empty set. A [request]/[response]/[header] is built by the caller as a
   literal (§2.2); introspection reads its attrs + child elements.

   RENDER NOTES (verified against the real binary): a header value renders
   QUOTED when numeric-looking (value='1') but BAREWORD for alphabetic
   (value=c); `headers` yields a node SEQUENCE rendered ([header …], [header
   …]); an absent header is the absence channel rendered () (empty
   node-set, §2.5); a present empty-valued header renders value='';
   body-bytes renders as a quoted string scalar ('hi'); an empty body is ''.
   The /@value path step that distinguishes a present-empty header ('') from
   an absent one (()) is applied to a BOUND result ([?let [= $h …] $h/@value])
   — a bare top-level `…/@value` is a parse error.

   SPEC CONTRADICTION (logged, fixtured to the binary): §3.1 says a bad/
   relative `base-url` at `client` → CXER4525. The impl reads opts only off
   element attrs, not the materialized {base-url: …} map, so `client` never
   inspects base-url and returns a valid [http-client] regardless. The
   binary is the oracle, so no CXER4525-at-client fixture is asserted; the
   real validation surfaces at `send` instead (a relative url= with no
   base-url → CXER4525, http-021). Recorded in SPEC-FINDINGS.
#]]

 [- §3.4 introspection — status / ok (PURE, response-only) -]

 [case id=http-001-status-200 level=core
  [tags http status pure]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/http']
[$http:status [response status=200]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
200
#]]
 ]

